  <description>
    <p>YouTube Music in a fast, native desktop app</p>
    <p>
      Built in Rust with egui and rodio. No Electron, no browser-based player, and around 130 MB of
      memory while playing.
    </p>
    <p>Features:</p>
    <p>
      <em>Listening</em>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your music feed: Listen again, Quick picks, your mixes (My Supermix, Discover Mix and more)
        and mood filters, refreshed each time you open a page.
      </li>
      <li>
        Explore and search: new releases, charts, and search across songs, albums, artists and
        playlists.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your library: liked songs, saved albums and playlists, history and uploads. Upload your own
        songs from the Uploads tab or by dropping files on the window. Turn on podcasts to see shows
        and episodes too.
      </li>
      <li>
        Song lists: drag songs to reorder the queue and your own playlists, or drop them on a
        playlist in the sidebar to add them. A find box narrows any list to the songs that match.
      </li>
      <li>
        Playback: queue, song radio, shuffle, repeat, autoplay and crossfade, with the next track
        prefetched for instant skips. Volume normalization, a 10-band equalizer with presets,
        playback speed that keeps the pitch (or lets it go with the speed, like a record), and a
        choice of output device.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sound quality: Opus at about 256 kbps with YouTube Premium, the best stream YouTube offers,
        and Opus at about 130 kbps without it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Songs, not music videos: an official music video plays as its album recording, without the
        intros and skits, or switch to the video itself in Now playing.
      </li>
      <li>
        Skip non-music parts: jumps over talking, skits and sponsor breaks in music videos, as
        marked by SponsorBlock.
      </li>
      <li>
        Explicit songs: marked with an E. Turn on Hide explicit songs to leave them out of lists,
        search, the queue and autoplay, and optionally use YouTube's Restricted Mode as well.
      </li>
      <li>
        Downloads: save any song as a tagged .m4a with cover art, at the best quality your account
        gets, with its lyrics inside (timed as LRC where the source has them). Albums and playlists
        can each go in their own folder. Saved songs are listed in Library › Downloads and play
        offline.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em>Lyrics and looks</em>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Karaoke lyrics: word-by-word lyrics drawn the way the Better Lyrics extension draws them,
        from every Better Lyrics source, with automatic fallback, romanization, translations and
        per-song timing offsets.
      </li>
      <li>
        Animated background: the album art drifts and pulses with the music behind everything. Go
        full-screen, or hide the lyrics to see just the artwork.
      </li>
      <li>
        30 themes: 27 rebuilt from the Better Lyrics community themes, such as Apple Music, Spicy,
        Spinning Record, reCover and Material 3, plus the app's own ytm-rs and Classic looks and
        Fluent, in the style of Windows 11. Each one restyles the whole app, in light or dark mode,
        with real frosted glass where the theme has it. Use any bundled or installed font.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your colours: the accent can follow the theme or your system, or be any colour you pick. A
        theme.conf file can set every colour in the app, for tools like wallust and pywal to write.
        Zoom makes everything bigger or smaller.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em>Together and across services</em>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Listen together: start a jam, share the code or link, and up to 16 people hear the same song
        at the same moment. The host decides whether guests can add songs or control playback.
      </li>
      <li>
        Transfer from Spotify: bring playlists and Liked Songs over to YouTube Music in the
        background. ytm-rs asks you when it isn't sure about a match and picks up where it left off
        if interrupted.
      </li>
      <li>
        Last.fm, ListenBrainz and Discord: scrobbling and Rich Presence.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em>Desktop</em>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        System media controls with a seekable timeline, taskbar thumbnail buttons, close to tray
        (Wayland too), song notifications while the window is in the background, a choice of page to
        open on, a collapsible sidebar and screen-reader-accessible controls.
      </li>
      <li>
        The app draws its own title bar on Windows, so the window is one surface, and on Linux
        desktops that draw none. On Windows 11, Mica or Acrylic can show behind the window.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your language: the interface comes in 20 languages, and song titles, shelves and charts can
        follow your language and country.
      </li>
      <li>
        Touch screens: swipe to scroll pages and lyrics, and swipe the player bar up to open Now
        playing.
      </li>
      <li>
        Mini player: shrink the window to a small strip with the song and controls (Ctrl+M), or try
        the experimental pill that sits in a corner and opens on hover.
      </li>
      <li>
        System-wide media shortcuts: play/pause, next, previous and volume work while another app is
        in front or the window is in the tray. Change them in Settings › Keyboard shortcuts.
      </li>
      <li>
        Automatic updates in the background for both the installed and portable versions, with
        release notes when an update is ready. Pick the Nightly channel for builds of the newest
        changes.
      </li>
    </ul>

  </description>

